# radar-clockwise-option — Implementation Plan

## 1. Goal

Add a new `radar.clockwise` component option (boolean) to the **radar coordinate system**, so that the
order in which indicator axes are laid out around the circle can be reversed.

Engineering statement:

* `RadarOption` (`src/coord/radar/RadarModel.ts`) gains `clockwise?: boolean`, defaulting to `false`.
* `Radar` (`src/coord/radar/Radar.ts`) gains a public `clockwise: boolean` field (sibling of the
  existing `startAngle`, `cx`, `cy`, `r`, `r0` fields), read in `Radar.prototype.resize()`.
* The per-indicator angle computation in `Radar.prototype.resize()` changes from a fixed
  `+idx * 2π / n` step to a signed `dirSign * idx * 2π / n` step, where `dirSign === -1` when
  `clockwise` is truthy (because `coordToPoint()` uses `y = cy - coord * sin(angle)`, i.e. *increasing*
  angle already means *counter-clockwise* on screen).
* The stale `// TODO clockwise` marker at the top of `src/coord/radar/Radar.ts` is removed.
* Indicator 0 must stay exactly at `startAngle` in both directions (no regression for existing charts,
  since the default `false` reproduces today's math bit-for-bit: `dirSign === 1`).
* Nothing in `src/component/radar/RadarView.ts` needs to change — see Findings §2.4 for the proof that
  label / axis-name placement is preserved.

## 2. Findings (current code)

### 2.1 The feature is an explicit, long-standing TODO

`/workspace/src/coord/radar/Radar.ts:20`

```ts
// TODO clockwise

import IndicatorAxis from './IndicatorAxis';
```

### 2.2 The angle of each indicator axis is hard-coded to one direction

`/workspace/src/coord/radar/Radar.ts:125-150` (`resize()`), verbatim:

```ts
    resize(radarModel: RadarModel, api: ExtensionAPI) {
        const refContainer = createBoxLayoutReference(radarModel, api).refContainer;

        const center = radarModel.get('center');
        const viewSize = Math.min(refContainer.width, refContainer.height) / 2;
        this.cx = numberUtil.parsePercent(center[0], refContainer.width) + refContainer.x;
        this.cy = numberUtil.parsePercent(center[1], refContainer.height) + refContainer.y;

        this.startAngle = radarModel.get('startAngle') * Math.PI / 180;

        // radius may be single value like `20`, `'80%'`, or array like `[10, '80%']`
        let radius = radarModel.get('radius');
        if (isString(radius) || isNumber(radius)) {
            radius = [0, radius];
        }
        this.r0 = numberUtil.parsePercent(radius[0], viewSize);
        this.r = numberUtil.parsePercent(radius[1], viewSize);

        each(this._indicatorAxes, function (indicatorAxis, idx) {
            indicatorAxis.setExtent(this.r0, this.r);
            let angle = (this.startAngle + idx * Math.PI * 2 / this._indicatorAxes.length);
            // Normalize to [-PI, PI]
            angle = Math.atan2(Math.sin(angle), Math.cos(angle));
            indicatorAxis.angle = angle;
        }, this);
    }
```

`idx * Math.PI * 2 / n` is always added, so indicator index always advances counter-clockwise on
screen. There is no way to flip it.

Screen-direction proof — `/workspace/src/coord/radar/Radar.ts:90-96`:

```ts
    coordToPoint(coord: number, indicatorIndex: number) {
        const indicatorAxis = this._indicatorAxes[indicatorIndex];
        const angle = indicatorAxis.angle;
        const x = this.cx + coord * Math.cos(angle);
        const y = this.cy - coord * Math.sin(angle);
        return [x, y];
    }
```

`y` is negated, so a *larger* angle moves the point counter-clockwise in screen space. Hence
`clockwise: true` ⇒ step sign `-1`.

### 2.3 The option does not exist in the model

`/workspace/src/coord/radar/RadarModel.ts:58-63` — `RadarOption` has `startAngle` but no `clockwise`:

```ts
export interface RadarOption extends ComponentOption, CircleLayoutOptionMixin {
    mainType?: 'radar'

    startAngle?: number

    shape?: 'polygon' | 'circle'
```

`/workspace/src/coord/radar/RadarModel.ts:178-190` — `defaultOption` likewise:

```ts
    static defaultOption: RadarOption = {

        // zlevel: 0,

        z: 0,

        center: ['50%', '50%'],

        radius: '50%',

        startAngle: 90,

        axisName: {
```

`/workspace/src/coord/radar/Radar.ts:45-57` — the coordinate-system instance exposes `startAngle` but
nothing for direction:

```ts
    cx: number;

    cy: number;

    r: number;

    r0: number;

    startAngle: number;

    private _model: RadarModel;
```

### 2.4 Nothing else has to change — verified consumers of `indicatorAxis.angle`

Exhaustive grep of `.angle` / `coordToPoint` / `getIndicatorAxes` consumers:

* `/workspace/src/component/radar/RadarView.ts:44-57` builds one `AxisBuilder` per indicator with
  `rotation: indicatorAxis.angle`, `position: [radar.cx, radar.cy]`, `labelDirection: -1`,
  `tickDirection: -1`, `nameDirection: 1`:

  ```ts
            const axisBuilder = new AxisBuilder(indicatorAxis.model, api, {
                axisName: axisName,
                position: [radar.cx, radar.cy],
                rotation: indicatorAxis.angle,
                labelDirection: -1,
                tickDirection: -1,
                nameDirection: 1
            });
  ```

  `AxisBuilder` derives every text alignment purely from `cfg.rotation` and the axis extent
  (`/workspace/src/component/axis/AxisBuilder.ts:588-616` `innerTextLayout`,
  `:1019-1047` `endTextLayout`, `:856-872` name positioning, `:913-940` name `graphic.Text`
  creation). It never assumes a rotation ordering across axes. Because the *set* of angles produced
  for `clockwise: true` is a mirror of the counter-clockwise set (for `n` indicators and
  `startAngle` s, `{s - k·2π/n}` normalized is the same modulo-2π lattice mirrored about `s`), every
  individual axis is laid out by the exact same code path with a legal rotation value. → **label
  placement is preserved by construction; no edit needed in `RadarView.ts`.**

* `/workspace/src/component/radar/RadarView.ts:129-165` (`_buildSplitLineAndArea`, polygon branch)
  builds ring polygons as `points.concat(prevPoints)` where `prevPoints` is the previous ring
  *reversed*. Reversing the global winding direction (which `clockwise` does) keeps outer/inner rings
  counter-wound relative to each other, so the even-odd/non-zero fill of `graphic.Polygon` is
  unaffected.

* `/workspace/src/chart/radar/radarLayout.ts:35-58` iterates indicator axes by index and calls
  `coordSys.dataToPoint(val, axisIndex)` — index-driven, direction agnostic.

* `/workspace/src/chart/radar/RadarSeries.ts:112`, `:145-146` — same, index-driven.

* `/workspace/src/coord/radar/Radar.ts:98-123` (`pointToData`) picks the closest axis by
  `Math.abs(radian - indicatorAxis.angle)`; this is symmetric in direction and needs no change.

### 2.5 Precedent for the option name / semantics

Other components already use a boolean `clockwise`:
`/workspace/src/coord/polar/AxisModel.ts:43` (`clockwise?: boolean;`),
`/workspace/src/component/polar/install.ts:43` (`clockwise: true,`),
`/workspace/src/chart/pie/PieSeries.ts:112`/`:220`,
`/workspace/src/chart/gauge/GaugeSeries.ts:134`/`:209`,
`/workspace/src/chart/sunburst/SunburstSeries.ts:129`/`:227`,
`/workspace/src/chart/chord/ChordSeries.ts:131`/`:308`.

Those default to `true`, but for radar the *existing* rendering is counter-clockwise, so the default
here **must be `false`** to keep every existing radar chart pixel-identical. (See Risks §6.1.)

